  "body": "MAINLINE-037b filed. 037's rigid shear-lag screen gives AF 163-2U's 250 F cure 1063 kPa, factor 0.227, but it cannot hold: the core would carry 47 times the in-plane force at which its walls yield. CR-1457, Hexcel and NASA TM-4585 give the faces the in-plane rigidity. On that reading the continuous bond carries no mismatch shear and the walls sit at 102-122 MPa (factor 2.24/1.87). A free core edge (unfilled splice, closeout) reads 1.17 typical, 0.83 minimum; W governs. The cure's larger cost is in the faces: 4.4 MPa of balanced compression passes through 028b's fixed 30:1 scarf, which binds at its 10 MPa screen, so 028b's usage goes from 0.972 to 1.052 (gamma 0.618). 037's floated scarf falls to 0.9925; any bond stress-free above 54 C does that. Lightest sourced fix, +0.12 kg: AF 163-2U's own 200 F datasheet cure (free edge 1.51/1.08) plus a 30.34:1 scarf by 028's sizing rule (usage 0.972). Room-temperature or mid-swing cures need no scarf change (0.990/0.982) but rest on projections. If 037's screen is kept, only a bond stress-free at 0.4-25.9 C passes. Matched cores (32-85 kg/m3) and RTV interlayers cannot float on mass. Noted, not taken: 028b's 30:1 scarf pins its faces; 30.44:1 alone reads 0.894. Thirteen NFI rows; a checker runs behind. The brief names no next job; the robotics seat sends it.",
